# Depends = engine hard requirements only (top-level `use` in the shipped
# code, per dist/config/sbom-deps.json / docs/reference/host-dependencies.md):
# Template Toolkit + MultiMarkdown are the processor core; everything else
# non-core is lazy-required. perl covers the core modules (JSON::PP,
# Digest::SHA, DB_File, ...).
Depends:
 perl,
 libtemplate-perl,
 libtext-multimarkdown-perl,
 ${perl:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
# Recommends = the packaged production runtime (SM142 FastCGI pool) and the
# lazily-loaded modules behind stock manager features (theme/layout install,
# zip download, remote includes, SMTP forms).
Recommends:
 libfcgi-perl,
 libfcgi-procmanager-perl,
 libarchive-zip-perl,
 libwww-perl,
 liburi-perl,
 libio-socket-ssl-perl,
Suggests:
 libnet-xmpp-perl,
Description: static site builder and manager - engine payload and CLI
 lazysite renders Markdown sites through Template Toolkit layouts, with a
 built-in manager UI, WebDAV/MCP publishing endpoints, forms, and auth.
 .
 This package ships the engine payload at /usr/share/lazysite (never
 touching any site tree), the `lazysite` host CLI (provision, upgrade,
 check, users, dev server), the site registry directory
 /etc/lazysite/sites.d, and the lazysite@.service systemd template unit
 that runs one persistent FastCGI pool per site. Sites are provisioned
 and upgraded from the payload AS THE SITE USER, so file ownership in
 the site tree is correct by construction.
 .
 Plain CGI remains the zero-dependency fallback; the FastCGI pool
 (libfcgi-perl, libfcgi-procmanager-perl) is the production shape.
